# VLA functional specification (model)

This is what the model computes, tick by tick, for the published configuration (`siliconperception/vla`,
run {{VERSION}}, a ConvDelta model). It specifies the function, not an implementation. `vla.py` is the reference:
where this text and the code disagree, the code is right and this text is a bug.

![One clock tick of the model](../figures/vla_{{VERSION}}.svg)

## Dimensions

| Symbol | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| S | 7 | grid side (positions P = S × S = 49) |
| C | 256 | channels of the working grid x, through every block |
| B | 7 | ConvDelta blocks, in series |
| H | 6 | memory heads per block at each grid position |
| d | 32 | key, query and value width of a head (each memory is d × d) |
| n | 3 | 3×3 convs per block |
| E | 256 | token embedding width (pythia-31m) |
| V | 50,304 | vocabulary (pythia-31m BPE), plus `<NUL>` |
| f | 25 Hz | tick rate in training; one token per tick |

## State

Everything carried from one tick to the next, 2,107,392 values in all, fixed in size:

| Part | Shape | Values |
|---|---|---|
| Per block b = 1 … 7: memories M_b | 49 positions × 6 heads × 32 × 32 | 301,056 |
| **Total** | 7 × 301,056 | **2,107,392** |

The memories are the whole state: there is no token history and no other register. The state never grows. At
inference it can be set to zero at the start of an example (a document, opened by `<NUL>`), and the evaluations
below do so, but it never has to be: in training it is never reset (see Training), as a robot perceives
continuously, so the model has learned to let go of an old context on its own.

## Notation

- RMS(z): z / √(mean over channels of z² + ε), ε = 1e-12, for the token embedding.
- σ: the logistic sigmoid. ⊙: elementwise product. Conv k×k: zero padding unless "unpadded" is stated.
- Every 1×1 conv acts at each grid position separately; only the k×k convs (k ≥ 3) mix neighbouring positions.

## One tick

Input: the current token id w_t (or `<NUL>`, "nothing this tick"). Output: the next-token distribution and the
sampled token.

### 1. Input grid

1. **Embed.** e = RMS(Embed(w_t)), with the frozen pythia-31m embedding (V → E = 256); e = 0 for `<NUL>`.
2. **Replicate.** The same vector e at all 49 grid positions.
3. **Mix channels.** x = Conv1×1_in(e), 256 → 256, at each position (so every position starts out equal; the
   3×3 convs' zero padding is what first tells the positions apart).

Only the current token enters. Everything the model knows about earlier tokens is in its memories.

### 2. Seven ConvDelta blocks

Each block b = 1 … 7 maps x (256 × 7 × 7) to x′ of the same shape, using and updating its memories M_b. All seven
have this structure, each with its own weights:

1. **Spatial mixing.** u = x + Conv1×1_mix(Z(x)), where Z is 3 × [Conv3×3 (256 → 256) → ReLU] (no normalization) and
   Conv1×1_mix (256 → 256) is linear, initialized to the identity. The residual goes around all three convs and
   the 1×1, so u can carry signed values.
2. **Projections**, at each position p, from u:
   - q, k, v = Conv1×1(u), 256 → 3 × 6 × 32: one query, key and value of width 32 per head; q and k are scaled
     to unit length per head (L2).
   - β = σ(g_β), α = exp(−2 · e^{λ_h} · σ(g_α)), with (g_β, g_α) = Conv1×1(u), 256 → 2 × 6 (weights and bias
     initialized to 0). β ∈ (0, 1) is the write strength. α ∈ (0, 1) is the decay; λ_h is a learned scalar per
     head, initialized so that e^{−λ_h} (the head's base window, in ticks) is log-spaced from 2 to 16,384 over
     the 6 heads.
3. **Gated delta rule**, for each position p and head h, on that position's memory M (32 × 32):

   M ← α · M · (I − β k kᵀ) + β v kᵀ

   That is: decay the whole memory by α, erase what it held at key k (in proportion β), and write v there.
   With unit k and β < 1 the erase step cannot grow M, so the memory stays bounded.
4. **Read.** o_h = M q, with the memory just written: 6 heads × 32 = 192 values per position.
5. **Output.** y = Conv1×1_out(o), 192 → 256 (weights and bias initialized to 0), and x′ = u + y.

The memories have no weights of their own: M is state, a fast weight written every tick. It is shaped only by
the projections feeding it.

### 3. Output and decoding

1. **Decode** the last block's x′ directly (no projection): a linear tree of unpadded convs, 3×3 (256 → 256,
   7×7 → 5×5), 3×3 (256 → 256, 5×5 → 3×3), 3×3 (256 → E, 3×3 → 1×1), then the frozen pythia-31m output layer
   (E → V), plus one trained logit for `<NUL>`. Embedding rows the tokenizer never uses are masked off.
2. **Emit.** Softmax, then min-p sampling: only tokens with at least p_min times the top token's probability
   are kept. p_min is stored with the weights; in training it is adjusted so that the model's own text,
   generated from `<NUL>`, scores a fluency of 100 (p_min = {{MINP}} at step {{STEP}}).
3. The sampled token is the next tick's input (in training, the dataset's next token is).

### Cost per tick

About 0.74 G multiply-accumulates per tick, 82 % of them in the 21 3×3 convs (28.9 M each). The 49 × 6 memory
updates and reads of a block take about 1.2 M. At 500 Hz that is 0.37 T multiply-accumulates per second, and
every tick does exactly the same work.

### Streaming on a chip

Every 3×3 conv can be computed row by row from a line buffer of a few rows of its input. The skips are cheap:
the one around a block's convs spans a single block, and the one around the memory read is per position. No
full copy of an activation grid has to be held from one layer to a later one.

## Parameters

| Part | Parameters | Trained |
|---|---|---|
| Input: Conv1×1_in (256 → 256) | 65,792 | yes |
| Per block: 3 × Conv3×3 (256 → 256) | 1,770,240 | yes |
| Per block: Conv1×1_mix (256 → 256) | 65,792 | yes |
| Per block: q, k, v (1×1, 256 → 576) | 148,032 | yes |
| Per block: β, α gates (1×1, 256 → 12) and λ | 3,090 | yes |
| Per block: Conv1×1_out (192 → 256) | 49,408 | yes |
| 7 blocks | 14,255,934 | yes |
| Decoder (tree and `<NUL>` logit) | 1,770,497 | yes |
| Token embedding | 12,877,824 | frozen |
| Output layer | 12,877,824 | frozen |
| **Total** | **41,847,871** (16,092,223 trained) | |

## The figure

[`docs/figures/vla_{{VERSION}}.svg`](../figures/vla_{{VERSION}}.svg) draws one clock tick (40 ms at 25 Hz) in two
panes, 1920 × 2160. Colours encode the kind of operation, the same in both panes: grey hatched = frozen
(embedding, output layer); violet = RMS norm and replication; blue = linear convs (1×1 or 3×3); green = conv 3×3
+ ReLU (no BatchNorm); yellow = the delta-rule memory (Δ) and its read; orange = the β, α gates; brown = L2
normalization; cyan = the decoder convs; pink = softmax and sampling. A box drawn like a D flip-flop (Q, D and a
clock notch) is state: a register written once per tick. Solid arrows are data and state paths; dashed arrows
carry the old state into an update.

### Pane A (top): the whole model

The subtitle line gives the configuration: `--lm` (text only), C 256, the 7×7 grid, 7 blocks, H 6 heads × d 32
per position, 25 Hz, 16.09 M trained parameters, a fixed state of 2,107,392 values, the input as this token
copied to every grid position, and training on 256-tick windows with the state never reset.

Left to right:

1. **text** enters at the left through an input mux.
2. **tok_embed (frozen)**, hatched grey: pythia-31m, 50304 × 256.
3. **RMS · replicate 1×1 → 7×7**, violet: the embedding scaled to unit RMS and copied to every position
   (labelled **256×7×7**).
4. **ctx_cat 1×1 256 → 256**, blue: the channel mix (input step 3).
5. **block 1 … block 7**, seven rounded light-blue boxes in series. Inside each, left to right: three green bars
   (the three conv 3×3 + ReLU) and a blue bar (the 1×1), a skip line from the block's input over them to a
   **(+)**, then a yellow circle **Δ** (the delta-rule memory) whose read is added in. Below each Δ is a
   flip-flop **S**, its memory state: a solid arrow from Δ down into S (the write) and a dashed arrow from S back
   up into Δ (the old state it updates). Under each block, **3 × conv, 1×1** names the mixing and
   **49×6×32×32** is the size of the block's memory. The caption reads "each block: u = x + 1×1(3 × (conv 3×3 +
   ReLU)), then x′ = u + (delta-rule memory Δ read)".
6. A dashed orange frame around block 2, "one block, zoomed in: pane B ▼", points to pane B.
7. The last block's output, the dot labelled **o**, goes straight to the decoder.
8. **text_dec**: three cyan bars, **3×3 7→5**, **3×3 5→3**, **3×3 3→1**, the decoder tree.
9. **lmhead 256 → 50304 (frozen)**, hatched grey.
10. **softmax, sample (+ \<NUL\>)**, pink. A line from it runs along the top back to the input mux, labelled
    "the sampled token → the next tick's input (training: the dataset's)".

Below the diagram, the **Legend** lists these colours, the (+) residual add, the flip-flop symbol ("D flip-flop
register, clocked every tick (S: never reset; it carries across documents)") and the two arrow styles.

### Pane B (bottom): one block

Its title says all 7 blocks have this structure, each with its own weights and memory; the subtitle gives x as
256×7×7 per tick, H = 6 heads of d = 32 at each of the 49 positions, that every operation is per position except
the 3×3 convs, and the base windows exp(−λ), learned, starting log-spaced over the heads from 2 to 16,384 ticks.

Left to right along the main line:

1. **x from below** (the previous block's output, or the input grid for block 1) splits: one branch goes
   through three green bars, **conv 3×3 · ReLU**, and a blue **1×1 conv (linear)**, in series; the other
   runs underneath as the skip, labelled "spatial mixing, per tick". They meet at a **(+)**. The sum is **u**.
2. From u, the main line runs straight right to a final **(+)** and on as **x′ to the next block**.
3. Below, u fans out into five 1×1 convs ("per position: 6 heads × 32"): **1×1 conv → q** (query) and **1×1
   conv → k** (key) in blue, each followed by a brown **L2** box; **1×1 conv → v** (value) in blue;
   **1×1 conv → β** ("write strength σ(·)") and **1×1 conv → α** ("decay exp(−2 exp(λ) σ(·))") in orange.
4. k, v, β and α enter the large yellow **gated delta rule** box: "S ← α · S (I − β k kᵀ) + β v kᵀ", "erase what
   k pointed at, write v there, decay the rest by α", "per position, per head: d×d = 32×32, about 3d² MACs each
   per tick", and in bold "no weights of its own: S is state (fast weights), learned only through the 1×1 convs
   feeding it".
5. Under it, the flip-flop **S**, "49 × 6 × 32×32 = 301,056", with a solid arrow in from the update and a dashed
   arrow, labelled **S (t−1)**, back into it.
6. The updated memory, **S (t)**, goes up to **read S q** (yellow), which also takes q from the L2 box along the
   top. The read goes to **1×1 out** (blue), then up, labelled **y**, into the final (+).
7. The panel **training vs the chip**, at the right: in training, everything before Δ runs on all 256 ticks of
   a window (64 rows) at once, and q, k, v, β, α for the window go to a chunked scan (the WY form of the delta
   rule) that gives every S q exactly; the state carries on between windows, and the gradient stops at a
   window's start. On the chip it runs one tick at a time: per block it holds S and does the convs, five 1×1
   projections, and 49 × 6 small 32×32 updates and reads, with the same results as training, by construction.

Pane B's legend repeats the colours used there and adds: L2 (q, k scaled to unit length per head, which keeps S
bounded), the gate formulas β = σ(·), α = exp(−2 exp(λ) σ(·)) with λ per head, (+) as the residual add, and the
note that S is never reset and carries across documents.

## Training (for reference)

- From scratch, to step {{STEP}}. A step is one tick (one token) for each of 64 rows, so {{TOKENS}} tokens in all,
  in {{UPDATES}} optimizer updates.
- Data: FineWeb sample-10BT, text only.
- Each row trains on windows of 256 consecutive ticks. Within a window the convs run on all ticks at once, and
  the memories are trained over the whole window by an exact chunked parallel scan. The state carries on from
  one window into the next, but the gradient stops at a window's start. Each block's activations are recomputed
  in the backward pass to save memory. After each update the window is run again with the new weights to give
  the state the next window starts from.
- **Document starts:** in training the state is never reset. When one document ends and the next begins, the
  previous, unrelated document's memories carry straight on, marked only by the `<NUL>` separator, as when a
  robot's perception jumps to a new scene, task or speaker. The model has to learn to let go of stale context
  itself, through α and β, which see the input. Evaluations reset at every document.
- Optimizer, no gradient clipping throughout; every ramp below is a half cosine over 20,000 steps:
  - to step 2,926,848: AdamW, β (0.9, 0.95), no weight decay, the learning rate rising from 0 to 3e-4, then held;
  - to step 3,232,512: the same, the learning rate lowered to 1e-4;
  - to step 3,716,096: plain SGD (no momentum, no weight decay), the learning rate mostly between 0.03 and 0.1
    (above about 0.13 training diverged; those attempts were discarded);
  - to step {{STEP}}: AdamW, β (0.9, 0.95), with fresh moment estimates, the learning rate rising from 0 to
    1e-3, then held; weight decay 0.1 from step 4,134,912.

  Cross-entropy is normalized per word, not per token.
- **Published weights:** an exponential moving average of the trained weights, θ̄ ← θ̄ + (θ − θ̄)/300 after every
  update, kept through the whole run, taken at step {{STEP}}. It removes most of the update-to-update noise:
  at {{STEP}} it scores about 0.2 nats per token lower on FineWeb than the weights it averages.
- Frozen: the token embedding and the output layer (pythia-31m).
- Numerics: the convs and the q, k, v projection run in bf16 on the GPU, the rest in TF32. In float64, the
  parallel scan equals one-tick stepping to about 1e-14.

## References

The model combines ideas from these papers:

- **Delta-rule fast-weight memory:** I. Schlag, K. Irie, J. Schmidhuber, *Linear Transformers Are Secretly Fast
  Weight Programmers* (2021), [arXiv:2102.11174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.11174).
- **Matrix-valued recurrent state, linear attention as an RNN:** A. Katharopoulos et al., *Transformers are RNNs:
  Fast Autoregressive Transformers with Linear Attention* (2020), [arXiv:2006.16236](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16236).
- **Gated delta rule (decay α with write strength β, L2-normalized q and k):** S. Yang, J. Kautz,
  A. Hatamizadeh, *Gated Delta Networks: Improving Mamba2 with Delta Rule* (2024),
  [arXiv:2412.06464](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06464).
- **Chunked parallel training of the delta rule (WY form):** S. Yang, B. Wang, Y. Zhang, Y. Shen, Y. Kim,
  *Parallelizing Linear Transformers with the Delta Rule over Sequence Length* (2024),
  [arXiv:2406.06484](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06484).
- **Data-dependent scalar decay per head:** T. Dao, A. Gu, *Transformers are SSMs: Generalized Models and
  Efficient Algorithms Through Structured State Space Duality* (2024), [arXiv:2405.21060](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.21060).
- **Recurrent state at every position of a conv grid:** X. Shi et al., *Convolutional LSTM Network: A Machine
  Learning Approach for Precipitation Nowcasting* (2015), [arXiv:1506.04214](https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.04214).
- **Residual blocks:** K. He et al., *Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition* (2015),
  [arXiv:1512.03385](https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385).
- **RMS normalization:** B. Zhang, R. Sennrich, *Root Mean Square Layer Normalization* (2019),
  [arXiv:1910.07467](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07467).
- **Tokenizer, frozen embedding and output layer, and the reference model:** S. Biderman et al., *Pythia: A Suite
  for Analyzing Large Language Models Across Training and Scaling* (2023), [arXiv:2304.01373](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01373).
- **Optimizer:** I. Loshchilov, F. Hutter, *Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization* (2017),
  [arXiv:1711.05101](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.05101).
- **Weight averaging:** B. T. Polyak, A. B. Juditsky, *Acceleration of Stochastic Approximation by Averaging*
  (1992), [doi:10.1137/0330046](https://doi.org/10.1137/0330046).
- **Training data:** G. Penedo et al., *The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at
  Scale* (2024), [arXiv:2406.17557](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.17557).
